\begin{theorem}[M2 --- Materialization is commutative]\label{thm:m2}
For fresh names $c_1, c_2$ and expressions $e_1, e_2$ depending
only on $L$'s attributes (and not on each other's outputs):
\[
\mu_{e_1 \to c_1}\, \mu_{e_2 \to c_2}(L)
\;=\;
\mu_{e_2 \to c_2}\, \mu_{e_1 \to c_1}(L).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each $r \in L$, LHS computes $e_1(r)$ and then $e_2(r)$, attaching
both; RHS computes $e_2(r)$ and then $e_1(r)$. Because $e_1$ and
$e_2$ depend only on $r$ and not on each other's intermediate
result, the two sequences yield the same extension $r \oplus \{c_1
\mapsto e_1(r), c_2 \mapsto e_2(r)\}$. The multisets are equal. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[M3 --- Materialization through projection]\label{thm:m3}
For fresh $c$ and expression $e$ depending only on attributes in $A$:
\[
\mu_{e \to c}\, \pi_A(L) \;=\; \pi_{A \cup \{c\}}\, \mu_{e \to c}(L).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $r \in L$, the LHS produces $\pi_A(r) \oplus \{c \mapsto
e(\pi_A(r))\}$. The RHS produces $r \oplus \{c \mapsto e(r)\}$ (using
the same $e$ since $e$ depends only on $A$), then projects to
$(A \cup \{c\})$. By the support of $e$, $e(r) = e(\pi_A(r))$, and
the resulting tuples agree. The freshness of $c$ ensures the
projection is well-formed. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[V1 --- Vector selections on distinct columns commute]\label{thm:v1}
For distinct vector-valued attributes $V_a, V_b$ and corresponding
bound parameters and thresholds:
\[
\sigma_{\lVert V_a - q_a\rVert < \tau_a}\,
\sigma_{\lVert V_b - q_b\rVert < \tau_b}(L)
\;=\;
\sigma_{\lVert V_b - q_b\rVert < \tau_b}\,
\sigma_{\lVert V_a - q_a\rVert < \tau_a}(L).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The composite predicate is $(\lVert V_a - q_a \rVert < \tau_a) \wedge
(\lVert V_b - q_b \rVert < \tau_b)$, which is equivalent to
$(\lVert V_b - q_b \rVert < \tau_b) \wedge (\lVert V_a - q_a \rVert <
\tau_a)$. By A1, the selection-composition identity, the two
orderings collapse to the same composite selection. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
V1 is a \emph{correctness} identity: the two plans produce the same
logical result. They are not interchangeable in \emph{cost}: the
order in which the two vector selections are evaluated affects how
many candidate rows each HNSW search returns, the cumulative HNSW
work, and the intermediate result-set sizes. The optimizer must keep
both orderings in the plan space and let the cost model choose
(Section~\ref{sec:cbo}).
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[J1 --- Vector selection pushdown through join]\label{thm:j1}
Where $V_a$ is an attribute of $L$ and $\psi$ is the join predicate:
\[
\sigma_{\lVert V_a - q \rVert < \tau}\, (L \bowtie_\psi M)
\;=\;
(\sigma_{\lVert V_a - q \rVert < \tau}(L)) \bowtie_\psi M.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Direct instantiation of A4 with the vector predicate as $\varphi$.
The result is the same multiset because $\lVert V_a(r) - q \rVert <
\tau$ is independent of any attribute of $M$. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[T1 --- Limit after order equals top-$K$]\label{thm:t1}
Where $O$ is a total ordering expression:
\[
\tau_O\, \Lambda_K(L) \;=\; \topK_O(L, K).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition, $\tau_O(L)$ produces the rows of $L$ in non-decreasing
order of $O$, with stable tie-breaking on remaining columns;
$\Lambda_K$ then takes the first $K$. The result is precisely the
$K$ smallest rows under $O$. The two plans are logically equal. The
interesting observation is \emph{physical}: the LHS in the algebra
may be implemented as \texttt{Sort} followed by \texttt{Limit}; the
RHS as \texttt{TopKHeap} with incremental bounds. They are equivalent
in result but very different in cost. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[G1 --- Selection pushdown through graph expansion]\label{thm:g1}
Where $\varphi$ references only attributes of $L$ (and not
attributes of the graph rule's traversal result):
\[
\sigma_\varphi\, \Gamma_{g, d}(L) \;=\; \Gamma_{g, d}\, \sigma_\varphi(L).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $R$ be the set of rows of $L$ satisfying $\varphi$; the seed set
of LHS is $L$, of RHS is $R$. Let $\mathrm{reach}(g, d, S)$ denote
the set of nodes reachable from a seed set $S$ by rule $g$ within
depth $d$. Monotonicity of reachability gives $\mathrm{reach}(g, d,
R) \subseteq \mathrm{reach}(g, d, L)$. The $\sigma_\varphi$ on the LHS
then removes any row whose origin row is not in $R$ (since
$\varphi$ depends only on the origin's attributes), so the two
multisets agree. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Derived identities}\label{sec:equational:derived}

By composing the identities, additional rewrites become available. We
name two that the optimizer uses directly:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{J1+M1 (vector selection before materialization).}
    Combining J1 and M1 allows a join-side vector selection to be
    pushed below a materialization that binds a distance for sorting:
    \begin{align*}
      \sigma_{\lVert V-q\rVert<\tau}\; \mu_{\lVert V-q\rVert\to d}\; (L \bowtie M)
      &= \mu_{\lVert V-q\rVert\to d}\;
         \sigma_{\lVert V-q\rVert<\tau}\;(L \bowtie M)
         \quad\text{(M1)} \\
      &= \mu_{\lVert V-q\rVert\to d}\;
         \bigl(\sigma_{\lVert V-q\rVert<\tau}(L) \bowtie M\bigr)
         \quad\text{(J1)}
    \end{align*}
    The plan now computes the distance only on the surviving $L$
    rows.
  \item \textbf{T1 (topK replaces $\tau + \Lambda$).} Whenever the
    algebra has $\tau_O\, \Lambda_K$, the CBO can replace the pair
    with a single \texttt{topK} physical operator
    (Section~\ref{sec:cbo}).
\end{itemize}

The algebra is closed under composition: the output of any operator
is a logical plan usable as input to any other. The schema rules
ensure that the attributes a downstream operator references are
either attributes of the input or have been introduced by a $\mu$ in
the pipeline. The rewrite rules documented in
Sections~\ref{sec:equational:inherited}--\ref{sec:equational:derived}
--- together with the standard axioms A1--A6 --- therefore suffice to
cover the optimization of all plans whose operators draw from the
inventory of Section~\ref{sec:algebra}.

\subsection{Non-identities and counterexamples}\label{sec:equational:nonident}

Some rules that look natural in a relational setting \textbf{do not
hold} in the extended algebra, and a third class --- physical
identities --- holds in the logical algebra but not in the physical
algebra. We document these explicitly so the optimizer knows not to
apply them. The same logical plan in two realizations can have
different outputs; the optimizer must treat this as a
\emph{cost} phenomenon, not a \emph{correctness} phenomenon.

\subsubsection{N1: Approximation breaks logical equivalence}

\begin{theorem}[N1 --- HNSW approximation breaks logical equivalence]\label{thm:n1}
Logical $\sigma_{\lVert V-q\rVert<\tau}(R)$ is exactly defined.
Physical $H_{V, q, \tau}(R)$ is \emph{approximate} --- it is missing
some true neighbours and (depending on \texttt{ef}) may include some
non-neighbours. A logical equivalence does not imply that two
physical realizations preserve it.
\end{theorem}

\begin{counterexample}\label{ce:n1}
Let $R$ consist of a single row $r = (\mathit{id}: 1, \vect{emb}:
\vect{v})$ with $\lVert \vect{v} - \vect{q} \rVert = 0.99\tau$ (just
under the threshold). An HNSW index on $\vect{emb}$ configured with a
small \texttt{ef} and an unfavourable entry point may fail to reach
$r$ during the graph walk, returning an empty result. A linear scan
over $R$ returns $\{r\}$. Both are valid physical implementations of
the same logical selection, and the logical identity
$H_{V,q,\tau}(R) = F(R) \cap \ldots$ (whatever implementation
identity) does not hold under approximation.
\end{counterexample}

\begin{remark}
The optimizer must not assume that two implementations of a logical
operator agree on output; it must model approximation as a
first-class \emph{cost dimension} with quantified
recall/precision, and it must let the cost-based search explore the
trade-off (Section~\ref{sec:cbo}).
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{N2: Similarity join asymmetry}

\begin{theorem}[N2 --- Similarity join asymmetry]\label{thm:n2}
Logically, the similarity joins
$R \bowtie_{\lVert V_a-V_b\rVert<\tau} S$ and
$S \bowtie_{\lVert V_b-V_a\rVert<\tau} R$ are equivalent. Physically,
the two sides are not interchangeable in cost.
\end{theorem}

\begin{counterexample}\label{ce:n2}
Let $|R| = 10^6$ with an HNSW index on $V_a$, and $|S| = 10^3$ with
no index. The plan ``HNSW on $R$ probed by each row of $S$'' issues
$10^3$ HNSW probes, each touching a small portion of the graph. The
reverse plan ``HNSW on $S$ probed by each row of $R$'' would require
either building an HNSW index on $S$ (extra cost) or issuing $10^6$
probes. The two logical plans are equivalent; the physical plans
have different costs by three orders of magnitude.
\end{counterexample}

\begin{remark}
The cost model must encode which side of a join has the index and
which has the probes. The RBO can generate \emph{both} the LHS and
RHS join orderings; only the CBO can decide which is cheaper.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{N3: Limit and order do not distribute over union}

\begin{theorem}[N3 --- Limit and order do not distribute over union]\label{thm:n3}
$\Lambda_K\, \tau_O\, (R \cup S) \;\neq\; \Lambda_K\, (\tau_O(R))
\cup \Lambda_K\, (\tau_O(S))$ in general.
\end{theorem}

\begin{counterexample}\label{ce:n3}
Let $R$ contain $100$ rows each with score $0$, and $S$ contain
$100$ rows each with score $10$. Let $K = 5$ and $O$ be the score
attribute. The LHS, $\Lambda_5\, \tau_O(R \cup S)$, returns the $5$
rows from $R$ (the top $5$ of the union by score). The RHS,
$\Lambda_5\, \tau_O(R) \cup \Lambda_5\, \tau_O(S)$, returns the $5$
rows from $R$ (all score $0$) \emph{plus} the $5$ rows from $S$ (all
score $10$), giving a $10$-row result. The two multisets differ.
\end{counterexample}

\begin{remark}
The identity $\Lambda_K\, \tau_O(L \cup M) = \Lambda_K\, \tau_O(L)
\cup \Lambda_K\, \tau_O(M)$, which would be convenient for
distributed execution, does not hold. The optimizer must compute
the union before applying the limit. This is a strong argument for
keeping the \emph{logical} plan compact and letting the
\emph{physical} planner manage the distributed execution
(Section~\ref{sec:cbo}).
\end{remark}

A related non-identity: $\tau_O(L \cup M) = \tau_O(L) \cup \tau_O(M)$
is formal only when the union of two ordered sequences is itself a
well-defined sequence --- i.e. when the user accepts that ``ordered
union'' of two ordered bags is the interleave, not the re-sort. In
practice, $\tau$ is almost always paired with $\Lambda$, and T1
already subsumes the case.

\subsection{Convergent rewriting}\label{sec:equational:convergent}

The equational theory of Sections~4.2--4.5 can be oriented into a
\textbf{rewriting system} and analyzed for termination and
confluence. This subsection presents the rewriting system, exhibits
a well-founded order that witnesses termination, enumerates the
critical pairs that arise from overlapping redexes, and concludes
by Newman's lemma that the RBO is confluent.

\paragraph{Orienting the identities.}
Each identity in Section~4.3 is oriented in the direction that moves
a plan \emph{toward} the optimized form (i.e.\ the form on which the
cost model of Section~\ref{sec:cbo} prefers to spend its work). The
orientated rules form an \emph{abstract rewriting system} (ARS) on
the set of logical plans, which we denote by $\to_{\mathrm{RBO}}$.
The optimizer of Section~\ref{sec:rbo} is precisely an interpreter
for $\to_{\mathrm{RBO}}$: each rule fires when its LHS pattern
matches a redex in the current plan.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Identity & LHS & RHS \\
\midrule
M1 & $\sigma_{c<\tau}\ \mu_{e\to c}(L)$ & $\mu_{e\to c}\ \sigma_{e<\tau}(L)$ \\
M2 & $\mu_{e_1\to c_1}\ \mu_{e_2\to c_2}(L)$ & $\mu_{e_2\to c_2}\ \mu_{e_1\to c_1}(L)$ \\
M3 & $\mu_{e\to c}\ \pi_A(L)$ & $\pi_{A\cup\{c\}}\ \mu_{e\to c}(L)$ \\
J1 & $\sigma_{\|V-q\|<\tau}(L \bowtie_\psi M)$ & $\sigma_{\|V-q\|<\tau}(L) \bowtie_\psi M$ \\
T1 & $\tau_O\ \Lambda_K(L)$ & $\topK_O(L, K)$ \\
G1 & $\sigma_\phi\ \Gamma_{g,d}(L)$ & $\Gamma_{g,d}\ \sigma_\phi(L)$ \\
V1 & $\sigma_a\ \sigma_b(L)$ & $\sigma_b\ \sigma_a(L)$ (bidirectional) \\
A1 & $\sigma_\phi\ \sigma_\psi(L)$ & $\sigma_{\phi\wedge\psi}(L)$ \\
A4 & $\sigma_\phi(L \bowtie_\psi M)$ & $\sigma_\phi(L) \bowtie_\psi M$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Orientation of the equational theory as a rewriting system.}
\label{tab:rbo-rules}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Termination.}
We exhibit an interpretation $w : \mathrm{Plans} \to \mathbb{N}$
that strictly decreases under every rewrite rule. Let
\[
w(P) \;=\; \alpha \cdot s(P) \;+\; \beta \cdot r(P) \;+\; \gamma \cdot p(P),
\]
where $s(P)$ is the number of $\sigma$ operations in $P$ that
reference a $\mu$-bound attribute (a ``blocking selection'' that can
be pushed below its $\mu$ by M1); $r(P)$ is the number of redundant
materializations in $P$, i.e. $\mu$ operations whose result attribute
is neither used in a downstream $\sigma$, in a $\tau$ ordering
expression, nor in another $\mu$; $p(P)$ is the number of $\pi$
operations that drop attributes needed by a downstream $\mu$ or
$\sigma$ (which M3 can rewrite to retain those attributes); and
$\alpha, \beta, \gamma \geq 1$ are constants chosen so that every
rewrite's left-hand side is strictly larger than its right-hand side.

For every oriented rule in Table~\ref{tab:rbo-rules}, the LHS
contributes at least one unit to one of $s, r, p$ that the RHS
eliminates or reduces, and the RHS preserves or decreases the other
terms. Combined with the trivial bound $w(P) \leq (\alpha+\beta+\gamma)
\cdot |P|$, strict decrease of $w$ under every rewrite yields
termination: the ARS has no infinite rewrite sequences.

\paragraph{Local confluence.}
Two rewrites can apply to the same plan in different ways when their
redexes overlap. We enumerate the critical pairs.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt]
  \item \textbf{M1 $\cap$ M1 (overlapping $\mu$ chains).}
    Apply M1 to two adjacent blocking selections in a chain
    $\sigma_{c_1<\tau_1}\, \sigma_{c_2<\tau_2}\,
    \mu_{e_2\to c_2}\, \mu_{e_1\to c_1}(L)$. The two ways (push
    $c_1$ first vs.\ push $c_2$ first) yield two reducts that are
    joined by A1 (selection composition, then reordering): the
    $\sigma$s collapse to $\sigma_{e_1<\tau_1 \wedge e_2<\tau_2}$,
    and the $\mu$s collapse to the same chain order by M2.
  \item \textbf{M1 $\cap$ J1 (selection on $L \bowtie M$).}
    A selection $\sigma_{\|V_a-q\|<\tau}$ sits above a join. M1
    rewrites it into $\mu_{\|V_a-q\|\to c}\, \sigma_{c<\tau}$;
    J1 pushes the $\sigma$ below the join. The two paths reach
    $\mu_{\|V_a-q\|\to c}\, (\sigma_{\|V_a-q\|<\tau}(L) \bowtie_\psi M)$.
  \item \textbf{G1 $\cap$ A4 (selection above $\Gamma$, on an
    attribute shared with the seed).} A $\sigma_\phi$ above a
    $\Gamma_{g,d}$ can be pushed below by G1, or split by A4 if
    $\phi$ references both the seed and the result. G1 dominates
    when $\phi$ references only the seed attribute; the cost model
    (Section~\ref{sec:cbo}) prefers G1. The two reducts agree on
    the resulting row set by the monotonicity argument of the G1
    proof (Section~4.3).
  \item \textbf{M2 $\cap$ V1 (commuting materializations vs.\
    commuting selections).}
    $\mu_{e_1\to c_1}\, \mu_{e_2\to c_2}\, \sigma_a\, \sigma_b(L)$
    admits both a M2 swap and a V1 swap. All four orderings are
    joined by the confluent closure of M2, V1, and A1.
  \item \textbf{T1 $\cap$ M1 (topK after a materialization-above-
    selection).} A pattern
    $\tau_{0.7 \cdot d_1 + 0.3 \cdot d_2}\, \Lambda_{10}\,
    \sigma_{d_1<\tau_1}\, \mu_{\|\mathit{emb}-q\|\to d_1}(L)$
    is a topK in the LHS position (T1 fires), or M1 fires first to
    push the $\sigma$ below the $\mu$, then T1 fires. Both reach
    $\topK_{0.7 d_1 + 0.3 d_2}\bigl(\mu_{\|\mathit{emb}-q\|\to d_1}\,
    \sigma_{\|\mathit{emb}-q\|<\tau_1}(L),\, 10\bigr)$.
\end{enumerate}

In every critical pair the two reducts can be further rewritten to a
common plan using the rules of Sections~4.2--4.4. This is local
confluence.

\paragraph{Newman's lemma.}
Newman's lemma \cite{newman1942} states that for an ARS,
\emph{termination + local confluence $\Rightarrow$ (confluence +
unique normal form)}. By the termination argument and the
critical-pair analysis above, we have both. Hence:

\begin{theorem}[RBO Confluence]\label{thm:rbo-confluence}
For any starting logical plan $P$ and any two sequences of RBO
rewrites $P \to^*_{\mathrm{RBO}} P^*$ and $P \to^*_{\mathrm{RBO}}
P^{**}$, there exists a plan $P^\infty$ such that $P^* \to^*
_{\mathrm{RBO}} P^\infty$ and $P^{**} \to^*_{\mathrm{RBO}} P^\infty$.
Moreover, the normal form $P^\infty$ is unique: it depends only on
$P$, not on the order in which rewrites are applied.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Termination holds by the weight function $w$ above. Local confluence
holds by the critical-pair analysis. The conclusion follows by
Newman's lemma.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Significance.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence} is the formal correctness guarantee
of the RBO. Two different optimization paths reach the same optimized
plan, independent of the order in which rules fire. This is a
property that heuristic, hand-tuned optimizers (e.g.\ the multi-path
recall orchestrators of Section~\ref{sec:intro}) cannot provide. It
is also a property that the CBO inherits ``for free'': the CBO's
search is over the unique canonical form produced by the RBO, so the
CBO's choice of physical realization is well-defined and reproducible.

The practical consequence is that the RBO can be implemented as a
simple greedy loop (apply any rule that decreases $w$ and matches a
redex) without losing correctness --- the order of rule application
does not affect the final logical plan. This simplifies the
optimizer implementation considerably and is a key reason for
choosing a rule-based architecture over a purely cost-driven search
(Section~\ref{sec:rbo}).

