% 6. From Algebra to Optimizer: RBO
% =====================================================================
\section{From Algebra to Optimizer: RBO}\label{sec:rbo}

The rule-based optimizer (RBO) is the first phase of the optimizer
pipeline. It operates on \textbf{logical plans} and applies the
identities of Section~\ref{sec:equational} to enumerate equivalent
plans. The output is a (typically modest) set of logically
equivalent plans that the CBO will then consider for realization.

\subsection{RBO role}

The RBO's job is \textbf{not} to pick a plan. Its job is to generate
a \emph{plan space} in which the CBO can search effectively.
Concretely, the RBO uses the equational theory to:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Apply selection pushdown} (A4, J1, G1) so that
    selective filters are evaluated early and reduce the input to
    expensive downstream operators.
  \item \textbf{Commute vector and classical selections} (V1, A1) so
    that the more selective predicate runs first.
  \item \textbf{Reorder materializations} (M2) so that independent
    materializations can be evaluated in any order --- this matters
    when the physical realizations have different costs (one HNSW
    scan, one BM25 scan, for example).
  \item \textbf{Expose topK opportunities} (T1) by collapsing
    $\tau_O\, \Lambda_K$ into a single \texttt{topK} operator that
    the physical planner can implement with a bounded heap.
\end{enumerate}

The RBO does \emph{not} decide which access path (HNSW vs. linear
scan, B-tree vs. heap) to use. It only reshapes the logical plan.

\subsection{Example transformations}

Returning to the $Q_1$--$Q_6$ example of
Section~\ref{sec:algebra}, the input logical plan is
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \Lambda_{10}\;
  \tau_{0.7\cdot d_1 + 0.3\cdot d_2}\;
  \sigma_{d_1<\tau_1 \wedge d_2<\tau_2}\; \\
& \mu_{\lVert\vect{emb\_alt}-\vect{q}_2\rVert\to d_2}\;
  \mu_{\lVert\vect{emb}-\vect{q}_1\rVert\to d_1}\;
  \sigma_{\mathit{subsystem}=\mathrm{`ft'} \wedge
  \mathit{ts}>\mathrm{now}()-30d}(\Mem).
\end{aligned}
\]
The RBO applies the following rewrites in order:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Selection composition} (A1):
    $\sigma_{d_1<\tau_1 \wedge d_2<\tau_2}$ becomes the composition of
    two $\sigma$s, one per predicate.
  \item \textbf{Selection--materialization commutativity} (M1), per
    predicate: each $\sigma_{d_1<\tau_1}$ is rewritten to
    $\mu_{\ldots\to d_1}\, \sigma_{\lVert\vect{emb}-\vect{q}_1\rVert<\tau_1}$.
  \item \textbf{Selection--selection pushdown} (A1 again, plus V1 for
    the vector part): the two $\sigma$s commute, and the one with
    the lower expected selectivity runs first (a heuristic we encode
    as a rule with a cost precondition).
  \item \textbf{Classical selection pushdown} (A4): the
    classical $\sigma$ on $\mathit{subsystem}$ and
    $\mathit{ts}$ is pushed to the base scan.
  \item \textbf{topK fusion} (T1): the trailing
    $\tau_{0.7\cdot d_1+0.3\cdot d_2}\, \Lambda_{10}$ is recognized
    as a \texttt{topK}.
  \item \textbf{Materialization commutation} (M2): the two
    independent $\mu$s can be evaluated in either order; the RBO
    records both plans.
\end{enumerate}

The output is a small set of logical plans, all guaranteed to be
semantically equivalent, but each with a distinct shape (filter
order, materialization order, topK vs. sort+limit). The CBO then
chooses among them.

\subsection{Plan space pruning}

Without the RBO, the CBO would have to consider every shape of every
plan: for $n$ joins, the search space is $O(n!)$; for $k$
materializations, the space is $O(k!)$ per join ordering; and so on.
The RBO prunes this space in two ways:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Equivalent classes.} The RBO groups plans into
    equivalence classes under the rewriting rules. The CBO need not
    consider plans within a class that are guaranteed by the RBO to
    be suboptimal (e.g. plans where a non-selective filter runs
    before a selective one).
  \item \textbf{Canonical form.} Each equivalence class has a chosen
    \emph{canonical form} (e.g. selections pushed as low as possible,
    materializations clustered, topK fused). The CBO enumerates
    realizations of the canonical form.
\end{itemize}

The effect is that the CBO's search is over \emph{plan shapes} and
\emph{physical realizations} jointly, but the \emph{number of
shapes} is small (typically 1--3 for the agentic-memory queries we
have explored).

\subsection{Style: Volcano/Cascades}

Our RBO is modeled on the Cascades framework \cite{graefe1995}.
Equivalence rules are first-class objects, organized by operator
pair (e.g. one rule for $\sigma \times \mu$, another for $\Gamma
\times \sigma$). A memo structure caches explored expressions and
their cost bounds. The optimizer is built around the
convergent-rewriting analysis of
Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}: the implementation is a
small loop that applies rules that decrease the weight function
$w$. The \emph{interesting} code is the rules, not the engine.

The key consequence: \emph{adding a new operator is adding rules}.
There is no central optimizer loop to modify. A contributor who
wants to support, say, a temporal range join, writes the operator,
the equivalence rules that reference it, and the cost-model entries
--- and the optimizer gains the new capability. This is the property
that makes the algebra the API.

\subsection{Plan space and RBO complexity}\label{sec:rbo:complexity}

The RBO is a \emph{program} over the equational theory of
Section~\ref{sec:equational}, and its behaviour is constrained by the
\emph{size} of the plan space and the \emph{number} of rewrites
required to reach the normal form. This subsection makes these
constraints explicit.

\paragraph{Plan space.}
For a query $Q$, define the \emph{plan space}
\[
\mathrm{Space}(Q) \;=\; \{ P : P \text{ is a logical plan reachable from } Q \text{ by } \to_{\mathrm{RBO}} \}.
\]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}, the plan space is finite, and
its extremal elements are the normal form of $Q$ (unique up to the
V1/G1 commutativity classes). The plan space is a DAG under
$\to_{\mathrm{RBO}}$, with $Q$ at the root and the normal form $Q^*$
at the leaves (or, more precisely, at the unique normal-form node
modulo the V1/G1 equivalence).

\paragraph{Size bound.}
Let $n$ be the number of operator occurrences in $Q$. The plan
space is bounded by
\[
|\mathrm{Space}(Q)| \;\leq\; c^n
\]
for a constant $c$ that depends on the rule set. The exponent
comes from three sources: (i)~the $n!$ permutations of $n$
independent materializations (M2); (ii)~the $k!$ permutations of
$k$ vector selections (V1); and (iii)~the $d!$ permutations of
$d$ graph expansion and join orderings (G1, A4). In the worst case,
every operator commutes with every other, and the bound is
$O(n!)$. In practice, the commutation relations are sparser: a
vector selection commutes with another only on disjoint column
references, and a materialization commutes with another only when
their expressions are independent. For typical agentic-memory
queries (5--15 vector columns, 1--3 materializations, 1 graph
expansion), the plan space is $O(10^3)$ to $O(10^5)$, not the
worst-case $O(15!)$.

\paragraph{RBO runtime.}
A na\"ive RBO that exhaustively applies all rules to all redexes
runs in time
\[
T_{\mathrm{RBO}} \;\leq\; |\mathrm{Space}(Q)| \cdot |R| \cdot t_{\mathrm{match}},
\]
where $|R|$ is the number of rules and $t_{\mathrm{match}}$ is the
time to match a rule's LHS against a redex. With the weight
function $w$ of Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}, the number
of rule applications is bounded by $w(Q)$, since every application
strictly decreases $w$. The effective runtime is
\[
T_{\mathrm{RBO}} \;\leq\; w(Q) \cdot |R| \cdot t_{\mathrm{match}} \;\in\; O(|Q|),
\]
linear in the size of the input plan.

\paragraph{Termination.}
By the termination argument of Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent},
the RBO terminates after a finite number of rewrites. The weight
function $w$ provides a worst-case bound: the number of rewrites is
at most $w(Q)$, and the number of distinct plans explored is at most
the size of the plan space. In practice, the RBO reaches the normal
form in $O(|Q|)$ rewrites for typical queries.

\paragraph{Pruning heuristics.}
The RBO can be made faster by two additional pruning heuristics,
both compatible with Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Rule preconditioning.} Each rule carries a
    precondition (e.g.\ ``M1 fires only on a $\mu$ whose materialized
    attribute is referenced in a downstream $\sigma$''). This reduces
    the number of redexes considered per rule and has no effect on
    correctness --- a rule with a failing precondition simply does
    not fire.
  \item \textbf{Memoization.} The RBO memoizes explored subplans in a
    Cascades-style memo structure, so a subplan that has been
    rewritten to its normal form is not re-explored. This is purely
    an efficiency measure; the result is unchanged.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{When is the plan space small?}
The plan space is small when the query has (i)~few vector columns
(so V1 contributes few orderings), (ii)~few materializations (so
M2 contributes few orderings), and (iii)~no graph expansion or only
one (so G1 contributes few orderings). For these queries, the RBO
terminates quickly and the CBO's search is over a small set of
canonical forms.

\paragraph{When is the plan space large?}
The plan space is large when the query has (i)~many correlated
vector columns and the predicate is selective, because V1 alone
creates $k!$ plan variants for $k$ vector selections; or (ii)~a
deep graph expansion $\Gamma_{g,d}$ with $d$ large, because G1's
effect compounds with the underlying selection-pushdown. For these
queries, the RBO must explore more of the plan space, and the
CBO's choice of physical realization matters more.

