\label{tab:cost-vectors}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Total cost.}
For a plan tree $T = \mathit{op}(T_1, \ldots, T_k)$,
\[
\mathrm{Cost}(T) \;=\; \mathrm{Cost}_{\mathit{op}}(T) \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathrm{Cost}(T_i) \;+\; \mathrm{io}_{\mathrm{materialize}}(T),
\]
where $\mathrm{io}_{\mathrm{materialize}}$ is the cost of
materializing intermediate results at materialization points (the
spill cost, which is incurred only when a subtree is forced to
materialize by, e.g., a non-pipelineable join). The cost is
recursive: the cost of a plan is the sum of its operators' costs,
each evaluated at the input cardinality determined by the plan's
structure.

\paragraph{CBO complexity.}
The CBO's search space is
\[
|\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{CBO}}(Q)| \;\leq\; |\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{RBO}}(Q)| \cdot |\mathrm{Phys}|^n,
\]
where $|\mathrm{Phys}|$ is the number of physical implementations
of each logical operator and $n$ is the number of operator
occurrences in the canonical form of $Q$. The exponential factor in
$n$ comes from the choice of physical implementation at each
operator; the factor $|\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{RBO}}|$ comes from the
RBO's plan space (Section~\ref{sec:rbo:complexity}). The CBO's
worst-case runtime is
\[
T_{\mathrm{CBO}} \;\leq\; |\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{RBO}}(Q)| \cdot |\mathrm{Phys}|^n \cdot t_{\mathrm{cost}},
\]
where $t_{\mathrm{cost}}$ is the time to evaluate the cost function
for one physical plan.

\paragraph{Pruning heuristics.}
The CBO applies three standard pruning techniques, all compatible
with correctness:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Dynamic programming over join orderings}
    (Selinger-style). For the $\bowtie$-fragment of the algebra, the
    CBO computes the optimal left-deep tree in $O(n^2)$ time using
    the recurrence of \cite{selinger1979}.
  \item \textbf{Greedy selection for vector predicates.} When the
    vector selections are estimated to be highly selective, the CBO
    evaluates them in selectivity order (most selective first),
    without exploring all $k!$ orderings.
  \item \textbf{Top-$K$ pruning for \texttt{ORDER BY}.} When $\tau$
    is followed by $\Lambda_K$, the CBO prunes physical plans whose
    cost exceeds the best top-$K$ cost found so far (per T1).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Open problem.}
When is cost-based optimization in the new algebra tractable? The
classical join-ordering problem is solvable in polynomial time for
left-deep trees (Selinger) but NP-hard for bushy trees
\cite{ibarakikameda1984}. With vector selections as first-class
operators (each contributing an $\mathit{ef}$-parameterized physical
realization), graph expansions (each contributing a
direction-parameterized physical realization), and materializations
(each potentially shared or distinct), the CBO's search space is
richer than the classical join-ordering problem. Identifying
polynomial subclasses --- for example, ``queries with at most one
graph expansion and at most two materializations are optimizable in
polynomial time'' --- is an open problem to which the algebra of
this paper provides the formal vocabulary.

