\section{An Algebraic Architecture}\label{sec:arch}

The contribution of this paper is the algebra of
Section~\ref{sec:algebra}, its equational theory of
Section~\ref{sec:equational}, and the optimizer that falls out of it
(Sections~\ref{sec:rbo}--\ref{sec:cbo}). This section makes explicit
the \emph{architectural} consequences of the algebraic foundation:
the layers a system must provide, the mapping from logical to
physical, the shape of the optimizer pipeline, and the scope
deliberately left out. We argue that the algebra \emph{implies} a
particular system architecture, and that this architecture is robust
to the choice of storage primitives and to the future evolution of
the cost model.

\subsection{From algebra to system: the design space}

A system that instantiates the MosaicOpt algebra must provide four
layers, each corresponding to a piece of the theory:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Storage layer.} Physical realizations of the column
    types: vector columns realized as HNSW indexes (or, in the
    future, learned indexes or product-quantization codes), text
    columns realized as BM25 inverted indexes, structured columns
    realized as B-trees or heap files, and graph relations realized
    as CSR or adjacency-map structures. Each storage primitive is a
    \emph{physical operator} in the algebra of
    Section~\ref{sec:cbo}; it implements one or more logical
    operators.
  \item \textbf{Surface layer.} The desugaring rules of
    Section~\ref{sec:sql:desugaring} compile SQL --- with the
    \texttt{IS NEAR}, \texttt{MATCHES}, and \texttt{IS REACHABLE}
    extensions --- into logical algebra plans. The surface layer is
    the \emph{only} component that sees SQL; from the optimizer's
    point of view, it consumes algebra plans.
  \item \textbf{Rule-based optimizer (RBO) layer.} Applies the
    equational rules of Section~\ref{sec:equational} to rewrite
    logical plans into their normal form
    (Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}). The convergent-rewriting
    property of Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent} guarantees
    termination and confluence: the RBO is \emph{well-behaved by
    construction}, and its implementation is a small loop that
    applies rules that decrease the weight function $w$.
  \item \textbf{Cost-based optimizer (CBO) layer.} Enumerates
    physical implementations of the logical normal form and selects
    the minimum-cost realization under the 4-tuple cost model of
    Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}. The CBO's search is over the
    canonical form (Section~\ref{sec:rbo:complexity}) and the table
    of logical-to-physical mappings
    (Section~\ref{sec:arch:bridge} below).
\end{enumerate}

The four layers are \emph{strictly ordered}: each layer consumes
only the output of the layer above it. This ordering is what makes
the system \emph{compositional}. Adding a new storage primitive
(e.g.\ a learned index) is a matter of adding a new physical operator
and a new mapping entry; the RBO and the CBO are unchanged. Adding
a new SQL clause is a matter of adding a new desugaring rule; the
algebra, RBO, and CBO are unchanged. Adding a new optimizer rule is
a matter of adding a rule to the equational theory; the cost model
and the executor are unchanged.

\subsection{Storage and the logical-to-physical bridge}\label{sec:arch:bridge}

The bridge between the logical algebra (what the optimizer
rewrites) and the physical algebra (what the executor runs) is a
\emph{table of logical-to-physical mappings} indexed by predicate
form. The table is the single point of contact between the
abstract algebra and the concrete storage layer.

