Three observations about this table:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{The table is small.} The number of physical
    realizations per logical operator is bounded by a small constant
    (typically 1--4): the choice between HNSW and linear scan,
    between $\mathrm{TopK}$ and $\mathrm{Sort}/\mathrm{Limit}$, and
    between the join algorithms. The CBO's search space
    (Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}) is therefore controlled.
  \item \textbf{The table is the only place a new storage primitive
    enters.} A learned index \cite{kraska2018}, a
    product-quantization code, or a more sophisticated HNSW variant
    is a new entry in the table, with a corresponding cost-vector
    entry in Table~\ref{tab:cost-vectors}. No optimizer change is
    required.
  \item \textbf{The table is auditable.} Because the table is a
    \emph{first-class data structure} (not code), it can be
    inspected, version-controlled, and tested. The optimizer's
    behaviour is fully determined by the table plus the equational
    theory, and both are inspectable.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The optimizer pipeline}

The optimizer is a three-stage pipeline that corresponds to the
three computational layers above:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{SQL $\to$ logical algebra.} The desugaring rules of
    Section~\ref{sec:sql:desugaring} compile the SQL surface into a
    logical plan. This stage is a \emph{function}; it has no
    decision points and is trivially auditable.
  \item \textbf{Logical $\to$ logical (RBO).} The equational rules of
    Section~\ref{sec:equational} are applied to the plan. The RBO
    terminates (Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent} termination)
    and is confluent (Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}), so the
    output is the \emph{unique normal form} of the input. The CBO
    can therefore treat the RBO's output as a single representative
    of the equivalence class; it need not consider the V1/M2/G1
    permutations.
  \item \textbf{Logical $\to$ physical (CBO).} The cost-based search
    enumerates physical realizations of the normal form, evaluates
    each under the cost vector of
    Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}, and selects the minimum. The
    CBO is the \emph{only} stage that involves search; the other
    two are functional.
\end{enumerate}

The pipeline's correctness argument is layered: stage~1 is correct
by construction (the desugaring is the definition of the SQL
surface); stage~2 is correct by
Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence} (the RBO preserves the logical
plan's semantics); stage~3 is correct by the bridge table of
Section~\ref{sec:arch:bridge} (each physical operator implements
its logical operator by definition). The executor, which runs the
physical plan, is correct by the physical operators' specifications.
The overall correctness is a \emph{composition} of these four
correctness arguments, and each is inspectable.

\subsection{What is left out}

The algebra of this paper is deliberately scoped to the read-only,
single-node, single-user setting. The following extensions are
explicitly out of scope and are the subject of the open problems
of Section~\ref{sec:open}:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Write paths.} How memory evolution (insert, update,
    delete) interacts with the algebra. A write algebra would need
    to model incremental updates to materialized views ($\mu$
    becomes stale when its underlying data changes) and to the cost
    model's statistical summaries (cardinality estimates drift).
    This is a substantial theoretical problem in its own right, and
    the current paper's contribution --- the read-only equational
    theory --- is the necessary foundation on which a write theory
    would build.
  \item \textbf{Multi-tenancy.} Per-user memory isolation, access
    control, and the corresponding cost-model terms. The natural
    extension is to add a \texttt{tenant} attribute and the
    corresponding selection-pushdown rules (A4 with
    \texttt{tenant = u} as the predicate). The equational theory of
    Section~\ref{sec:equational} applies with no changes; the cost
    model of Section~\ref{sec:cbo} gains a tenant-cardinality term.
  \item \textbf{Distribution.} How the algebra extends to a
    distributed setting (sharding, replication, network cost). The
    non-identity N3 --- that limit and order do not distribute over
    union --- is a particular obstacle for distributed
    \texttt{topK}; a partition-aware topK algorithm is an open
    research direction (Section~\ref{sec:open:distributed}).
  \item \textbf{Calibration of the cost model.} How to estimate the
    parameters in the cost vector (cardinality, selectivity, HNSW
    \texttt{ef}/$M$ trade-offs, BM25 $k_1$/$b$). This is empirical
    work --- the \emph{physics} of the algebra --- and is not a
    theoretical contribution in the PODS sense.
\end{itemize}

These exclusions are deliberate. The algebra is the \emph{central}
contribution; the cost model and the executor are the
\emph{necessary} accompaniments; the write path, multi-tenancy,
distribution, and calibration are \emph{future} work that the
algebra makes possible but does not yet contain. A theoretical paper
that attempted to do all four would dilute the
equational-theory contribution that is the paper's core.

\section{Related Theoretical Work}\label{sec:related}

The MosaicOpt algebra sits at the intersection of classical
relational algebra, algebraic extensions of the relational model,
the theory of equational rewriting, and the cost-based optimization
literature. This section surveys the most directly relevant prior
work in each of these threads and locates MosaicOpt's contribution
precisely. The goal is not to enumerate all related work but to
identify the \emph{lineage} of the algebra and to make clear what
is novel.

\subsection{Classical relational algebra}

The lineage of MosaicOpt begins with Codd's relational algebra
\cite{codd1970}, which showed that a small set of operators ---
selection, projection, Cartesian product, union, set difference, and
rename --- is sufficient to express any first-order query.
Subsequent work refined the operator set (adding explicit $\bowtie$
for theta-join, grouping/aggregation, ordering, and limits) and
crystallized in SQL. Date's textbook synthesis \cite{date2003} and
Ullman's \cite{ullman1988} established the relational algebra as
the \emph{lingua franca} of database theory.

The second foundational pillar is \textbf{query optimization}.
Selinger's cost-based optimizer for System~R
\cite{selinger1979} established the template that nearly every
commercial RDBMS still follows: enumerate alternative orderings
and access paths, estimate cost under a cardinality model, pick
the cheapest. The \textbf{Volcano} extensible query evaluation
framework \cite{graefe1994} and the \textbf{Cascades} optimizer
\cite{graefe1995} generalized this to a \emph{rule-based} outer
loop driving a \emph{cost-based} inner search, in which equivalence
rules over logical operators are first-class objects, and the
optimizer is itself extensible by adding rules.

The insight of Cascades --- that the algebra is the API --- is the
direct ancestor of MosaicOpt's design. A new operator is shipped
with its equivalence rules and cost model, and the optimizer gains
new capabilities without any central rewrite. MosaicOpt inherits
this property.

\subsection{Algebraic extensions of the relational model}

MosaicOpt is not the first proposal to extend the relational
algebra. The literature is rich; we highlight the most directly
relevant threads.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Nested relational algebra.} The nested relational
    algebra \cite{thomasfischer1986} extends Codd's algebra with
    set-valued attributes, enabling expression of object-oriented
    queries without a join. MosaicOpt's first-class materialization
    is in the spirit of the nested algebra's power to compute and
    bind complex values, but the focus is different: nested algebra
    is about \emph{structuring} complex values, while MosaicOpt is
    about \emph{binding} computed values (vector distances, BM25
    scores) for downstream use.
  \item \textbf{Object-relational algebra.} Beech's extension
    \cite{beech1989} and the SQL-99 object-relational model added
    user-defined types and methods to the relational substrate.
    MosaicOpt's typed vector/text/graph columns are in the same
    spirit --- a ``richer type system on top of the relational
    model'' --- but the algebraic treatment is more disciplined: a
    typed column is a \emph{first-class predicate} and a
    \emph{first-class materialization expression}, not just a
    method call.
  \item \textbf{Temporal algebra.} Tuzhilin and Clifford's
    \cite{tuzhilinclifford1990} temporal algebra and Snodgrass's
    \cite{snodgrass1995} temporal extensions add time-varying
    attributes and time-aware predicates to the relational model.
    MosaicOpt's graph-expansion operator $\Gamma$ is in the same
    spirit --- extending the model to a dimension (graph) that
    classical algebra does not capture --- but the dimensions are
    different. The equational theory of $\Gamma$ (G1) is the
    analogue of the temporal-algebra identities.
  \item \textbf{Semi-structured and XML algebra.} The
    semi-structured algebra \cite{fernandezsimeonwadler2000} and
    the various XPath/XQuery algebras add a \emph{tree} data model.
    The structural recursion of these algebras is similar in
    spirit to MosaicOpt's $\Gamma$ (which recurses over a graph
    relation), but the equational theory is different: the
    semi-structured algebras emphasize \emph{bisimulation} and
    \emph{structural equivalence}, while MosaicOpt emphasizes
    \emph{selection pushdown through graph expansion} (G1).
\end{itemize}

These extensions share MosaicOpt's pattern of ``extend the algebra,
prove identities''. MosaicOpt's contribution is to extend in a
\emph{new direction} (multi-modal: text + vector + graph, in the
context of agentic memory) and to provide, in one place, a
\emph{SQL surface} and an \emph{equational theory} that the prior
extensions did not.

\subsection{Equational theories in query optimization}

The classical identities of relational algebra --- selection
pushdown (A4), join commutativity, projection pushdown (A5) --- have
been part of the optimizer's arsenal since System~R. What MosaicOpt
adds is (i)~a \emph{new set of identities} (M1--M3, V1, J1, T1, G1)
for the extended operators, and (ii)~an explicit
\emph{convergent-rewriting} analysis
(Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}) that proves the extended
rewriting system is terminating and confluent.

The convergent-rewriting analysis draws on a classical literature
that pre-dates databases:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Newman's lemma} \cite{newman1942}: termination +
    local confluence $\Rightarrow$ confluence. This is the formal
    foundation of the rewriting-system analysis in
    Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}.
  \item \textbf{Huet's work on confluence} \cite{huet1980}:
    critical-pair analysis as a sufficient condition for local
    confluence. We use critical-pair analysis to enumerate the
    overlapping-redex cases in Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}.
  \item \textbf{The Terese textbook} \cite{terese2003}: a
    comprehensive treatment of abstract rewriting systems. The
    orientation of the equational theory into a rewriting system
    (Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}) follows the textbook's
    pattern of \emph{orienting an equational theory into a reduction
    system and proving its confluence}.
\end{itemize}

In the database literature, the closest prior work is Atzeni and
Torlone's \cite{atzenitorlone1996} treatment of view maintenance
as algebraic rewriting. Their setting is different (view
maintenance vs.\ query optimization), but the \emph{pattern} is the
same: orient an equational theory, prove confluence, and exploit
the normal form to drive a transformation.

MosaicOpt's convergent-rewriting analysis is, to our knowledge, the
first such analysis for a relational algebra that includes vector,
text, and graph operators.

\subsection{Cost-based optimization complexity}

The complexity of cost-based optimization is a classical subject.
Selinger et al.\ \cite{selinger1979} gave an $O(n^2)$ dynamic
programming algorithm for left-deep join orderings. Ibaraki and
Kameda \cite{ibarakikameda1984} showed that the join-ordering
problem for \emph{bushy} trees is NP-hard. Pellenkoft,
Galindo-Legaria, and Kersten \cite{pellenkoft1996} studied the
complexity of join ordering with outer joins and other extensions.

MosaicOpt's CBO (Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}) inherits this
complexity. The join-ordering subproblem is identical to the
classical one; the additions are (i)~vector selections as
first-class operators (each contributing an
$\mathit{ef}$-parameterized physical realization), (ii)~graph
expansions (each contributing a direction-parameterized realization),
and (iii)~materializations (each potentially shared or distinct).
The CBO's search space is therefore richer than the classical one;
identifying polynomial subclasses is an open problem
(Section~\ref{sec:open:complexity}).

More recent work on cost-based optimization for hybrid systems
includes the learned optimizer literature (Balsa, CostFormer) and
the cardinality estimation literature. These are \emph{physical}
and \emph{empirical} contributions --- not theoretical
contributions to the equational theory of the algebra --- and are
orthogonal to MosaicOpt.

\subsection{Hybrid query algebra (recent)}

The last decade has seen a sustained effort to bring vector and
relational together, but with very few \emph{theoretical}
contributions. The systems are numerous; the algebras are few.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Vector-aware RDBMS extensions} ---
    PostgreSQL's \texttt{pgvector}, DuckDB's \texttt{vss}, Oracle
    AI Vector Search --- add a vector type, an ANN index, and
    vector distance predicates. Integration is at the
    function-call level. There are no first-class identities for
    vector selection pushdown, no cost model for approximation, no
    principled way to rewrite a hybrid query. These are
    \emph{systems}, not \emph{theories}.
  \item \textbf{Purpose-built hybrid stores} --- Pinecone, Weaviate,
    Qdrant, Milvus, Vespa --- are vector-first, with metadata
    filtering bolted on. Their optimization story is pragmatic, not
    foundational. Their planners have rewrites, but the rewrites
    are not exposed as named identities.
  \item \textbf{Multi-modal query languages} --- SQL/MM, SPARQL with
    full-text extensions, Cypher, and various research prototypes
    --- mix graph, text, and (in a few cases) vector. SPARQL has a
    careful algebraic foundation, but its fusion with vector
    predicates is still an open area. Cypher is graph-first; SQL/MM
    is multi-modal but does not address vector similarity or ANN.
  \item \textbf{The MosaicOpt project} --- is, to our knowledge, the
    first to provide a \emph{unified algebraic foundation} for
    text, vector, and graph in the context of agentic memory, with
    a \emph{proven equational theory}
    (Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent},
    Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}) and a \emph{formal cost model}
    (Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}).
\end{itemize}

The common pattern in the prior systems: every system mixes the
modalities by \emph{fusing scores at the application layer}, not by
\emph{extending the algebra that defines what counts as an answer}.
MosaicOpt's contribution is to make the fusion \emph{first-class in
the algebra} --- a materialization expression is a syntactic
construct, the optimizer sees it, the cost model accounts for it,
and the equational theory of Section~\ref{sec:equational} applies
to it.

\subsection{What this paper does not cover}

Three conscious exclusions bound the scope of the contribution.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{We do not propose a new physical data structure.}
    HNSW, B-trees, and inverted indexes are taken as external; the
    paper's contribution is the \emph{algebraic} use of these
    primitives, not their internal design. A learned-index
    primitive \cite{kraska2018} would be a drop-in replacement in
    the bridge table of Section~\ref{sec:arch:bridge}; the algebra
    is unchanged.
  \item \textbf{We do not propose a new learning-based optimization.}
    CostFormer, Balsa, and the learned-cardinality literature are
    \emph{empirical} contributions to the \emph{physical} layer. The
    MosaicOpt contribution is at the \emph{logical} layer: the
    algebra, its equational theory, and the cost-model
    \emph{framework}. A learned optimizer can be plugged in to
    \emph{instantiate} the cost model of
    Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}; the algebra and equational
    theory are unchanged.
  \item \textbf{We do not propose a new transaction model.} The
    algebra is read-only. Transactional extensions (writes,
    isolation levels, multi-version concurrency) are out of scope
    and are addressed in Section~\ref{sec:open:write}.
\end{itemize}

These exclusions are not weaknesses; they are \emph{scope choices}
that keep the contribution focused on the algebraic foundation. The
algebra is the paper's central contribution, and the paper argues
that the algebra, by itself, is a useful and complete theoretical
contribution.
