\section{Open Theoretical Problems}\label{sec:open}

The algebra of Section~\ref{sec:algebra}, the equational theory of
Section~\ref{sec:equational}, the optimizer of
Sections~\ref{sec:rbo}--\ref{sec:cbo}, and the architectural sketch
of Section~\ref{sec:arch} are the contributions of this paper. They
are not the \emph{end} of the story: each layer opens new
theoretical problems that the algebra makes accessible but does not
yet solve. This section enumerates the most directly consequential
open problems, framed in the vocabulary the paper has introduced.

\subsection{Complexity of cost-based optimization in the new algebra}\label{sec:open:complexity}

Classical join ordering is solvable in $O(n^2)$ for left-deep trees
\cite{selinger1979} but NP-hard for bushy trees
\cite{ibarakikameda1984}. The CBO of
Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity} adds three new dimensions:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Vector selections as first-class operators.}
    Each vector selection has an $\mathit{ef}$-parameterized
    physical realization, contributing a continuous (rather than
    discrete) choice to the CBO's search. The CBO's search is no
    longer over a finite space of physical operators per logical
    operator.
  \item \textbf{Graph expansion as a first-class operator.}
    Each $\Gamma_{g,d}$ has a direction-parameterized physical
    realization, and the cost depends on the \emph{graph density}
    at the expansion point, which is not known a priori.
  \item \textbf{Materialization as a first-class operator.}
    Each $\mu$ may be \emph{shared} with another materialization
    (a single distance computation used in two places) or
    \emph{distinct}. The CBO must decide sharing.
\end{itemize}

The complexity of optimal plan selection in this richer setting is
open. We conjecture NP-hardness in the general case (because the
sharing-vs-distinct choice is a partition problem), and conjecture
polynomial-time subclasses for queries that bound the number of
graph expansions, the depth of materialization sharing, and the
cardinality of physical realizations per operator. Identifying
these subclasses --- and proving the boundary between tractable and
intractable --- is a central open problem.

\subsection{Cost model calibration}

The cost vector of Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity} has parameters
--- cardinality estimates, HNSW $\mathit{ef}/M$ trade-offs, BM25
$k_1/b$ --- that must be \emph{calibrated} against the data. For
the algebraic theory, the cost is a \emph{symbolic} function over
the plan's structure; for a concrete system, it must be
\emph{instantiated} with measurements (statistical summaries,
recall/precision curves, selectivity histograms).

The calibration is the \emph{physics} of the algebra: it depends
on the data distribution, the workload, and the storage primitives.
It is empirical work, not theoretical, but it is a critical open
problem because a poorly calibrated cost model leads to a CBO that
makes worse decisions than no CBO at all. The algebra's
contribution is to \emph{frame} the calibration problem precisely:
the cost vector has four components, and each component's
calibration is an independent subproblem.

\subsection{Adaptive optimization}

The cost model of Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity} is \emph{static}:
it is evaluated once, at planning time, and the resulting plan is
executed. In practice, the cost model is wrong: cardinality
estimates drift, HNSW recall varies with the query, and BM25
selectivity is hard to predict from small samples.
\textbf{Adaptive optimization} --- re-planning at well-defined
plan points based on observed cardinalities --- is a classical
approach \cite{graefe1994}.

The open problem in the MosaicOpt setting is \emph{how adaptive
optimization interacts with the algebra's convergent rewriting}
(Section~\ref{sec:equational:convergent}). When the CBO re-plans
mid-execution, it must respect Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}: the
re-planned logical plan must behave as a ``refinement'' of the
original plan, not a replacement. Two research questions arise:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Re-planning safety.} If the CBO switches to a
    different physical subplan mid-execution, is the switch
    \emph{safe} in the sense that the logical plan's semantics is
    preserved? (The answer is yes, by the bridge table of
    Section~\ref{sec:arch:bridge}; the \emph{proof} is the open
    problem.)
  \item \textbf{Re-planning convergence.} Does re-planning preserve
    the RBO's normal-form property? If the CBO switches to a
    subplan that is \emph{not} in the RBO's normal form, must it
    later re-rewrite? (We conjecture yes, and the rewriting is
    local to the switched subtree.)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Distributed execution}\label{sec:open:distributed}

The algebra is single-node. Extending to a distributed setting
requires (i)~a model of network cost in the cost vector
(Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}), (ii)~a theory of
\emph{partition-aware} physical operators (e.g.\ partition-aware
$\mathrm{TopK}$ that respects N3), and (iii)~an extension of the
equational theory to reason about partitioning.

The challenge is that graph expansion $\Gamma$ is harder to
parallelize than vector selection
$\sigma_{\lVert V-q\rVert<\tau}$: a graph expansion must traverse
edges, which cross partition boundaries; a vector selection can be
partitioned by the vector index itself. The algebra should make
the difficulty \emph{visible} to the cost model: the cost of
$\Gamma_{g,d}$ is a function of the partition structure, not just
the graph size.

\subsection{Write paths and memory evolution}\label{sec:open:write}

The algebra is read-only. A write algebra would need to model:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Incremental updates to materializations.} When a
    base row changes, every $\mu$ that depends on it must be
    invalidated or recomputed. The equational theory of
    Section~\ref{sec:equational} gives the \emph{semantics} of the
    materialization; the \emph{incremental maintenance} of the
    materialization is a separate (and harder) problem.
  \item \textbf{Index updates.} HNSW and inverted indexes must be
    kept consistent with the base data. The interaction between
    incremental index updates and the cost model's statistical
    summaries is a subtle problem.
  \item \textbf{Cost model drift.} The cost model's parameters
    (cardinality, selectivity) drift with the data. A write
    algebra must specify when and how the cost model is
    re-calibrated.
\end{itemize}

The interaction between writes and the algebra is the largest open
problem in the paper, and is likely the subject of follow-up
theoretical work.

\subsection{Privacy, security, and the algebra}

Agentic memory contains user data. Access control, differential
privacy, and the algebraic treatment of privacy are open problems.

A natural direction is to extend the algebra with \emph{selection
by capability} --- e.g.\ $\sigma_{\mathrm{ACL}(user) = \mathrm{read}}$
--- and to develop the corresponding equational theory for ACL
predicates (a re-formulation of A4 with the ACL predicate as
$\phi$). Differential privacy can be framed as a
\emph{transformation} of the cost vector: the $\mathrm{approx}$
component of Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity} becomes a privacy
budget $(\varepsilon, \delta)$, and the CBO's constraint becomes a
privacy constraint rather than a recall constraint.

The algebra provides the vocabulary to formulate these problems;
the solutions are open.

\subsection{Beyond first-order: higher-order, recursive, and aggregate queries}\label{sec:open:aggregate}

The MosaicOpt algebra is \emph{first-order} in the Codd sense.
Three extensions are open:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Higher-order queries.} ``For all memory units that
    mention $X$, find the one most similar to $Y$'' is a
    second-order query that the current algebra does not directly
    express. A natural extension is to add a $\forall$ quantifier
    over memory units, with the corresponding equational theory.
  \item \textbf{Recursive queries.} Recursive queries (e.g.\
    transitive closure) are expressed via $\Gamma$ in the current
    algebra. The equational theory of Section~\ref{sec:equational}
    covers the case when $\Gamma$ is \emph{terminating} (the depth
    bound $d$ is finite). When the depth is unbounded, the
    algebra's monotonicity properties still apply, but the
    equational theory must be re-proved for the least-fixed-point
    semantics.
  \item \textbf{Aggregate queries with vector predicates.} ``The
    average distance of the $K$ nearest neighbours'' is an
    aggregate over a vector selection. The current algebra does not
    support aggregates over $\mu$ directly; a
    grouping-aggregation extension is the natural next step.
\end{itemize}

These extensions are not in the paper, but the algebra is the
\emph{foundation} on which they would build.

\subsection{Closing}

The argument of this paper is simple: agentic memory is
multi-modal, multi-modal is a query problem, query problems benefit
from foundations, and the right foundation is an extension of
relational algebra. The algebra is small, the rewrite rules are
provable, the optimizer's convergence is provable
(Theorem~\ref{thm:rbo-confluence}), the cost model is formal
(Section~\ref{sec:cbo:complexity}), and the architectural
implications are explicit (Section~\ref{sec:arch}). The open
problems of Sections~\ref{sec:open:complexity}--\ref{sec:open:aggregate}
are the natural next steps for a research program that builds on
the foundation this paper provides.

